  [1mSecond fork:[0m how does [38;5;153mapp.js[39m actually get the settings, given [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads it as a plain [38;5;153m<script>[39m today.

  - [1mSecond [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag + a global[0m — [38;5;153msettings.js[39m loads before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m and sets one namespaced global (e.g.
    [38;5;153mwindow.AppSettings[39m). Buys: nothing about the loading model changes; [38;5;153mindex.html[39m still works by double-clicking the
    file. Costs: a global, and load order becomes load-bearing (mitigated by the script tag order being right there in
    the HTML). Cheap to change later.
  - [1mSwitch to ES modules[0m — [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m, [38;5;153msettings.js[39m uses [38;5;153mexport[39m, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m uses [38;5;153mimport[39m. Buys: real
    module boundary, no globals, the modern default. Costs: browsers block ES module imports over [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so opening
    [38;5;153mindex.html[39m directly stops working — you'd need a local server to run the page at all. Cheap to reverse mechanically,
    but it changes how you run the thing.

  Worth saying: [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m never touch the API endpoint, so I'm scoping this to the browser app only
  and not trying to make one settings module serve both the Node and browser sides. Say so if you'd rather it be shared.

  [1mMy recommendation: the script tag + global.[0m The only consumer is [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, and a one-line [38;5;153m<script>[39m addition keeps this
  repo's "open the HTML and it works" property, which the ES-module route silently takes away. If a bundler ever
  arrives, converting is a mechanical edit to two files.
